WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.920 --> 00:00:08.200
GUFF: OK, good evening, everyone. And welcome
to tonight's Council panel on U.S.-Russia

00:00:08.200 --> 00:00:14.610
relations on the eve of the Sochi Winter Olympics.
We have for you tonight an all-star panel

00:00:14.610 --> 00:00:21.600
of Russia experts across the spectrum, and
let me briefly introduce them to you.

00:00:21.600 --> 00:00:28.170
To my left is Ambassador John Beyrle. John
is the most recent -- before Mike McFaul -- U.S.

00:00:28.170 --> 00:00:37.100
ambassador to the Russian Federation, 2008-2012,
very active in the reset under President Obama.

00:00:37.100 --> 00:00:43.429
Dr. Angela Stent, she's the director and -- for
the Center of Eurasian, Russian and Eastern

00:00:43.429 --> 00:00:46.480
European Studies at Georgetown.

00:00:46.480 --> 00:00:52.559
And Ambassador and Dr. Steve Sestanovich,
whom all of you know from his role here at

00:00:52.559 --> 00:01:01.719
the Council. I'm clearly the most under-titled
person on this panel tonight. And so with

00:01:01.719 --> 00:01:06.189
that degree envy, let me welcome our panelists
tonight.

00:01:06.189 --> 00:01:12.170
I also want to give a warm welcome to the
CFR members around the nation, around the

00:01:12.170 --> 00:01:18.890
world who are plugging into us tonight through
the live meeting stream. So welcome to everyone

00:01:18.890 --> 00:01:19.890
there.

00:01:19.890 --> 00:01:25.390
And I would just like to mention one housekeeping
note. Our next meeting here at the Council

00:01:25.390 --> 00:01:30.640
is with Karl Eikenberry, Jeffrey Bader, and
Michael Green on what to do about tensions

00:01:30.640 --> 00:01:39.640
in Asia, same time, same place tomorrow, 5:30
here at the Council. I would like to mention,

00:01:39.640 --> 00:01:47.060
also, tonight's session is on-the-record.

00:01:47.060 --> 00:01:54.659
Let's start with the general topic. The Sochi
Olympics are coming up, and it gives us a

00:01:54.659 --> 00:02:01.420
very time for pause to take a look at the
state of U.S.-Russian relations. Angela, your

00:02:01.420 --> 00:02:12.310
book is just out, and so I'd like to ask you
to kick off. How would you describe today

00:02:12.310 --> 00:02:15.450
the state of relations at this point between
the U.S. and Russia?

00:02:15.450 --> 00:02:21.400
STENT: Thank you very much, Drew, and I'm
delighted to be here. As usual, it's complicated.

00:02:21.400 --> 00:02:28.769
You have the trifecta of Snowden, Syria and
Sochi, and they all illustrate different things

00:02:28.769 --> 00:02:35.549
about this complicated, as I said, partnership.
Edward Snowden, that caused real, serious

00:02:35.549 --> 00:02:41.019
problems in U.S.-Russian relations. The White
House was very angry that he was given political

00:02:41.019 --> 00:02:45.880
asylum in Russia. President Obama canceled
a summit with President Putin, and I think

00:02:45.880 --> 00:02:50.590
it's one of the reasons why neither he, nor
the vice president, nor the first lady are

00:02:50.590 --> 00:02:55.480
going to attend the opening ceremonies at
the Sochi games. So that shows a bilateral

00:02:55.480 --> 00:02:57.910
relationship that has many problems.

00:02:57.910 --> 00:03:03.420
But Syria, this is an area, it's a multilateral
area where we're working quite well with the

00:03:03.420 --> 00:03:07.099
Russians. We can maybe go into the details
of how that happened. The Russians took the

00:03:07.099 --> 00:03:12.299
initiative. It enabled us to work with them
and ward off any kind of military attack,

00:03:12.299 --> 00:03:18.970
so both with the chemical weapons, disarmament,
and now in Geneva, we work quite well together.

00:03:18.970 --> 00:03:24.050
And then there's Sochi, and there again in
Sochi, it's a mixed picture. We are working

00:03:24.050 --> 00:03:28.319
with the Russians on the security problems.
We're not working as well with them as we

00:03:28.319 --> 00:03:33.390
might like. I think both sides are wary about
sharing too much intelligence information

00:03:33.390 --> 00:03:38.140
with each other in dealing with the terrorist
threat, but we're working with them. Our athletes

00:03:38.140 --> 00:03:42.980
are going there. We will be competing. We'll
send a lower-level delegation, but still this

00:03:42.980 --> 00:03:46.060
is an area where we have done things together.

00:03:46.060 --> 00:03:51.450
And so I think it presents a picture that
has been characteristic for the last 22 years,

00:03:51.450 --> 00:03:57.040
since the Soviet Union collapsed, which is
on some areas, we can and must work with the

00:03:57.040 --> 00:04:02.090
Russians. We're fated to work with them on
areas like Syria, Iran, the broader Middle

00:04:02.090 --> 00:04:07.700
East, and on other areas, particularly bilateral
areas where we criticize them for what they

00:04:07.700 --> 00:04:13.170
do domestically and vice versa. It's very
hard to work with them.

00:04:13.170 --> 00:04:23.480
GUFF: John, you worked on the original reset,
and maybe you can shed some light on what

00:04:23.480 --> 00:04:29.580
were the areas on which the U.S. and Russia
found common interests and what were the areas

00:04:29.580 --> 00:04:32.350
where you just agreed to disagree?

00:04:32.350 --> 00:04:38.860
BEYRLE: Well, I think as Angela notes in her
book, resets, then detentes kind of come and

00:04:38.860 --> 00:04:43.400
go in the U.S.-Soviet and U.S.-Russia relationship.
Just in my 30 years of diplomacy, I think

00:04:43.400 --> 00:04:45.010
I can count about three or four detentes...

00:04:45.010 --> 00:04:46.620
SESTANOVICH: I think Angela says there were
four.

00:04:46.620 --> 00:04:47.620
BEYRLE: There were four, OK.

00:04:47.620 --> 00:04:50.700
SESTANOVICH: There were three initiated by
a U.S. presidents, one by Putin.

00:04:50.700 --> 00:04:51.700
STENT: Right.

00:04:51.700 --> 00:04:56.330
BEYRLE: So I think the question is, really,
what is it that makes a reset happen? What

00:04:56.330 --> 00:05:01.250
are the preconditions that allow Russia and
the United States or the Soviet Union and

00:05:01.250 --> 00:05:06.220
the United States to actually find common
ground? When I arrived in Moscow as ambassador

00:05:06.220 --> 00:05:13.130
in 2008, I found the relationship to be about
as tense and frayed as I've ever experienced

00:05:13.130 --> 00:05:19.260
it in my career, and this was a month before
Russian forces went into Georgia.

00:05:19.260 --> 00:05:26.980
I think mostly for economic reasons, as a
consequence of the economic boom that Russia

00:05:26.980 --> 00:05:33.680
enjoyed between about 2003 and 2008, the Russian
elite, the Kremlin had convinced itself, really,

00:05:33.680 --> 00:05:38.460
that it didn't need the West anymore. And
it had even kind of conceived a narrative

00:05:38.460 --> 00:05:44.280
that the West was responsible for some of
the problems that Russia had encountered.

00:05:44.280 --> 00:05:54.430
So by sheer chance, when I arrived, it coincided
with President Medvedev's arrival on the scene

00:05:54.430 --> 00:06:01.311
and, I'd say, more importantly the election
of a new U.S. president, Barack Obama, which

00:06:01.311 --> 00:06:06.200
allowed the Russians to turn the page and
to drop the litany of grievances that they'd

00:06:06.200 --> 00:06:07.910
conceived against the Bush administration.

00:06:07.910 --> 00:06:14.080
But the most important reason that the reset
happened was not political, but economic.

00:06:14.080 --> 00:06:20.050
It was the economic crisis that hit Russia
in 2008-2009, worse than any other emerging

00:06:20.050 --> 00:06:25.600
economy. The bottom dropped out of the Russian
stock markets. The ruble lost 10 percent of

00:06:25.600 --> 00:06:30.120
its value. Russia, which had been clipping
along at 7 percent growth for 6 or 7 years

00:06:30.120 --> 00:06:33.370
in a row, suddenly had negative 10 percent
growth.

00:06:33.370 --> 00:06:39.810
And what that did was give ammunition to the
economic reformers in the Russian elite who

00:06:39.810 --> 00:06:46.630
had been arguing that it was time for the
economy to actually diversify itself and become

00:06:46.630 --> 00:06:53.540
more modern, more innovative. President Obama
wanted to be a part of that. We saw this in

00:06:53.540 --> 00:07:01.500
the Obama administration as an opportunity,
and we were fortunate, I would say, in that

00:07:01.500 --> 00:07:08.610
President Medvedev at that time had several
advisers who were not only convinced that

00:07:08.610 --> 00:07:13.411
the kind of change in the Russian economy
was -- it was time for it to happen, but they

00:07:13.411 --> 00:07:15.690
actually knew how to work the system.

00:07:15.690 --> 00:07:21.850
And I would say the most important outcome
of that was the WTO accession by Russia. That

00:07:21.850 --> 00:07:26.981
would not have happened if Russia hadn't found
itself in these dire economic straits. The

00:07:26.981 --> 00:07:33.680
other two issues I'd say that probably were
the biggest successes or the best outcomes

00:07:33.680 --> 00:07:41.000
of the reset were the START treaty, continued
the nuclear builddown by both countries, and

00:07:41.000 --> 00:07:47.990
the Afghanistan agreement, whereby the Russians
actually agreed to help with the resupply

00:07:47.990 --> 00:07:55.870
of American forces in Afghanistan, all things
that Putin agreed on, but all three issues

00:07:55.870 --> 00:08:02.680
that President Medvedev and his advisers pushed
through to find a common cause with the Obama

00:08:02.680 --> 00:08:03.790
administration.

00:08:03.790 --> 00:08:14.440
GUFF: Steve, having mentioned these four resets,
why is it that we keep doing this dance over

00:08:14.440 --> 00:08:18.840
and over? Why is it that the relationship
with Russia seems to be a triumph of hope

00:08:18.840 --> 00:08:19.840
over experience?

00:08:19.840 --> 00:08:24.300
SESTANOVICH: Well, I think the main reason
is that Russia hasn't decided how to align

00:08:24.300 --> 00:08:33.919
itself. It is uncomfortable with the idea
of -- of integration into the West. And it

00:08:33.919 --> 00:08:45.050
is disinclined just to think that its ordinary
disagreements with the United States are clashes

00:08:45.050 --> 00:08:48.029
merely of national interest.

00:08:48.029 --> 00:08:58.190
For Putin in particular, there is a kind of
civilizational dimension to his -- his estrangement

00:08:58.190 --> 00:09:06.769
from the West, which is -- I think one of
the reasons that the relationship has taken

00:09:06.769 --> 00:09:14.550
on the prickly quality that it has, going
beyond just personality clashes, I think for

00:09:14.550 --> 00:09:23.240
Putin, Putin has a different conception of
how the world ought to work. He treats sovereignty

00:09:23.240 --> 00:09:37.899
as something semi-holy. And for him, the assertion
of Russian will is part of establishing Russian

00:09:37.899 --> 00:09:40.310
identity in the modern world.

00:09:40.310 --> 00:09:47.100
I mean, it's a -- this is not just a set -- and
here I think we may have left this out in

00:09:47.100 --> 00:09:53.110
the accounts which otherwise I completely
agree with that Angela and John have given

00:09:53.110 --> 00:09:59.209
-- there is a kind of ideological dimension
to Putin's -- the way in which he's distanced

00:09:59.209 --> 00:10:08.529
himself from the West. It's a principle. He
doesn't want to be part of the American block,

00:10:08.529 --> 00:10:13.450
and he doesn't think that these are just ordinary
disagreements.

00:10:13.450 --> 00:10:19.730
The biggest one is, of course, this whole
issue of how the West relates to political

00:10:19.730 --> 00:10:26.660
change in other countries, the idea that the
West encourages reform and transformation

00:10:26.660 --> 00:10:35.019
is for him a -- is really anathema. And that,
I think, is the deepest kind of problem that

00:10:35.019 --> 00:10:36.019
Russia has with us.

00:10:36.019 --> 00:10:37.019
GUFF: Is this...

00:10:37.019 --> 00:10:38.379
STENT: Could I just add something to that?

00:10:38.379 --> 00:10:39.379
GUFF: Yeah.

00:10:39.379 --> 00:10:43.220
STENT: I think that the -- we and the Russians,
if you look over -- again, over the period

00:10:43.220 --> 00:10:47.670
-- just at the 22 years, never mind going
back -- have a very different idea about what

00:10:47.670 --> 00:10:52.350
a good and productive relationship would look
like. And for the Russians -- and that's in

00:10:52.350 --> 00:10:57.140
my book -- it's really the idea of, particularly
since Putin came to power, of having something

00:10:57.140 --> 00:11:03.209
which one of my Russian colleagues described
as an equal partnership of unequals. It's

00:11:03.209 --> 00:11:08.420
for the U.S. to treat Russia as an equal,
even understanding that in many ways we aren't,

00:11:08.420 --> 00:11:14.350
at least since the Soviet collapse, and for
us accepting, as Steve said, what Russia is

00:11:14.350 --> 00:11:19.620
and not trying to persuade them that they
should become more like us, whereas I think

00:11:19.620 --> 00:11:23.829
our idea of what a productive relationship
with Russia is, is working with a Russia that

00:11:23.829 --> 00:11:27.040
more or less shares our values. And I think
that's a fundamental problem.

00:11:27.040 --> 00:11:31.930
BEYRLE: Yeah, I would -- if I could, I would
try to boil that down to a kind of paradigm

00:11:31.930 --> 00:11:37.980
that we identified when we were all working
on this relationship back in the late '90s

00:11:37.980 --> 00:11:42.209
and early 2000s. It seemed to us at that time
that Russia always wanted to have a special

00:11:42.209 --> 00:11:49.870
relationship with the United States. The specialness,
strategic partnership, detente, whatever it

00:11:49.870 --> 00:11:54.860
was, in a way, validated the sense of self-worth
in the Kremlin, to a certain degree, at that

00:11:54.860 --> 00:11:55.860
time.

00:11:55.860 --> 00:11:59.680
I think that really has changed. And when
I was back in Moscow earlier last year with

00:11:59.680 --> 00:12:07.210
all the former U.S. ambassadors and Russian
ambassadors to the United States, Foreign

00:12:07.210 --> 00:12:14.240
Minister Ivanov -- sorry, Lavrov -- said to
us, Russia really doesn't want a special relationship

00:12:14.240 --> 00:12:18.589
with the United States anymore. Russia wants
a normal relationship with the United States.

00:12:18.589 --> 00:12:24.939
And I think part of the problem is our getting
used to the fact that Russia really does not

00:12:24.939 --> 00:12:29.930
measure its sense of self-worth or place in
the world by the level of the relationship

00:12:29.930 --> 00:12:33.870
that it has with the United States. And I
think that's underlying a lot of some of the

00:12:33.870 --> 00:12:35.449
tensions that we feel now.

00:12:35.449 --> 00:12:42.459
GUFF: Steve, you mentioned this unique Russian
view of itself and its place in the world.

00:12:42.459 --> 00:12:49.959
How much of that goes back to Russia trying
to define its identity in terms of grand Russian

00:12:49.959 --> 00:12:59.680
destiny, its -- its messianic place in the
world, from the struggles between Slavophiles

00:12:59.680 --> 00:13:07.379
and Westerners? Is this part of a long wave
of historic cycles? Or is this just Mr. Putin's

00:13:07.379 --> 00:13:11.640
view of the world and where Russia fits in
according to hard-headed realistic goals?

00:13:11.640 --> 00:13:15.700
SESTANOVICH: Well, great historians have written
about this, and great Russian historians,

00:13:15.700 --> 00:13:16.700
too, and...

00:13:16.700 --> 00:13:17.700
GUFF: And great panelists have, Steve.

00:13:17.700 --> 00:13:25.139
SESTANOVICH: I'm not going to say any of that
is wrong, but I think right now, we're mostly

00:13:25.139 --> 00:13:31.720
dealing with the -- with Russia's adjustment
to the Soviet collapse, and then the sense

00:13:31.720 --> 00:13:40.249
of vindication that they've taken from rebuilding
themselves in their economy and society as

00:13:40.249 --> 00:13:45.209
a kind of working international player in
the past 10 years.

00:13:45.209 --> 00:13:54.470
So we can do a lot of grand cultural interpretation
and, you know -- this is a good group for

00:13:54.470 --> 00:14:02.690
that -- but I think the immediate issues that
-- that have dogged the relationship have

00:14:02.690 --> 00:14:07.560
-- may have a slightly narrower character
than that.

00:14:07.560 --> 00:14:14.970
Putin is thinking constantly about whether
or not the United States is promoting regime

00:14:14.970 --> 00:14:20.990
change in the former Soviet Union. In the
'90s, as John says, that was something that

00:14:20.990 --> 00:14:26.339
everybody accepted in a way. We didn't call
it regime change. We were talking about promoting

00:14:26.339 --> 00:14:33.089
reform. Clinton administration talked about
having an alliance with reform. In just the

00:14:33.089 --> 00:14:39.240
way that John and Angela have described, Putin
feels that was demeaning, we've gotten past

00:14:39.240 --> 00:14:48.310
it, we don't need it, and he feels that his
own historical standing is confirmed by rejecting

00:14:48.310 --> 00:14:49.310
it.

00:14:49.310 --> 00:14:52.680
BEYRLE: I think it's also worth noting that
-- I mean, we talk a lot about Putin and the

00:14:52.680 --> 00:14:58.670
Kremlin or the Russians, and we're not talking
about one thing. I mean, there are people

00:14:58.670 --> 00:15:05.560
in Russia now in the elite with power who
really do think that Russia's fate is tied

00:15:05.560 --> 00:15:09.660
up in closer association with the West, or
at least in a greater economic integration

00:15:09.660 --> 00:15:16.721
with Western institutions. And they are kind
of locked in a battle with Revanchist or -- I

00:15:16.721 --> 00:15:23.490
think Brzezinski called them imperial nostalgics
-- who always view the outside world with

00:15:23.490 --> 00:15:28.730
some sense of suspicion or even as a hostile
force.

00:15:28.730 --> 00:15:33.869
And clearly, we're never part of that debate
as Westerners, but we have a huge stake in

00:15:33.869 --> 00:15:38.139
how it turns out. And the best that we can
try to do is identify the people that we see

00:15:38.139 --> 00:15:42.800
on the right side of the debate, the debate
that takes Russia into a more integrated way

00:15:42.800 --> 00:15:48.149
of dealing with the outside world, because
this is the 21st century. Russians make about

00:15:48.149 --> 00:15:54.420
35 million trips abroad on average now. The
Internet is not censored in Russia.

00:15:54.420 --> 00:15:59.529
So the idea that the imperial nostalgics build
this mythology of a Russia under siege, building

00:15:59.529 --> 00:16:05.559
walls to keep the outside world at bay doesn't
work in this century. And I think, you know,

00:16:05.559 --> 00:16:08.370
the tides of history are on the side of the
reformers.

00:16:08.370 --> 00:16:12.189
SESTANOVICH: John is absolutely right about
this. There are plenty of Russians who do

00:16:12.189 --> 00:16:14.889
still want a special relationship with the
United States...

00:16:14.889 --> 00:16:15.889
BEYRLE: Or with Brussels.

00:16:15.889 --> 00:16:23.220
SESTANOVICH: ... who do view their progress
according to how they integrate with the West.

00:16:23.220 --> 00:16:25.550
There are plenty of Russians who want to be
in the E.U.

00:16:25.550 --> 00:16:32.339
BEYRLE: That's right, some of whom have run
for president before, like Mr. Prokhorov.

00:16:32.339 --> 00:16:33.339
SESTANOVICH: Yep.

00:16:33.339 --> 00:16:39.449
GUFF: But I want -- at the same time, we're
talking about integration with the West, integration

00:16:39.449 --> 00:16:45.910
with the rest of the world, but yet the major
part of government policy, the Kremlin policy

00:16:45.910 --> 00:16:56.399
is building the Eurasian trade and economic
and political sphere of influence at -- how

00:16:56.399 --> 00:17:03.480
realistic, Angela, do you think the goal of
Eurasian economic trade and political space

00:17:03.480 --> 00:17:04.480
is?

00:17:04.480 --> 00:17:08.610
STENT: I mean, it seems -- you know, five
years ago, I think we would have thought none

00:17:08.610 --> 00:17:14.330
of this is likely. Today, I think one can
question it more. Obviously, if you look at

00:17:14.330 --> 00:17:18.340
what's happening in Ukraine, nobody knows
where it's going, but this is clearly an instance

00:17:18.340 --> 00:17:23.640
where, on the one hand, it looks as if Russia,
you know, at least for the time being, has

00:17:23.640 --> 00:17:26.020
gotten the Ukrainian government to agree to
closer integration.

00:17:26.020 --> 00:17:30.840
I mean, at the moment, you have a customs
union with Kazakhstan and Belarus. You have

00:17:30.840 --> 00:17:37.260
Armenia that's going to join. I doubt that
Putin can create this Eurasian union, which

00:17:37.260 --> 00:17:42.130
will be a sort of Soviet Union-lite. But I
do think that there are profound problems

00:17:42.130 --> 00:17:47.440
of -- in really all of the post-Soviet countries,
they all suffer from the same kind of syndrome,

00:17:47.440 --> 00:17:53.260
which are these kind of corrupt, rather opaque
governments, and where you may have popular

00:17:53.260 --> 00:17:57.570
movements for more democracy, and the ties
that bind with Russia may be stronger than

00:17:57.570 --> 00:17:58.570
we thought they would be.

00:17:58.570 --> 00:18:03.760
So I don't think there will be the creation
of another Eurasian union, but I think Putin

00:18:03.760 --> 00:18:10.290
will do all he can to make sure that a country
like Ukraine doesn't have closer association

00:18:10.290 --> 00:18:15.990
with the European Union and that there will
be -- that this will be still a different

00:18:15.990 --> 00:18:18.380
bloc and it will look towards Russia.

00:18:18.380 --> 00:18:23.510
SESTANOVICH: I think one fact that just drives
home what Angela is saying is, four of the

00:18:23.510 --> 00:18:29.970
five Central Asian countries trade more with
China than with Russia. The Ukrainians trade

00:18:29.970 --> 00:18:37.210
as much with Europe as with Russia. The idea
that the Russians have that their economic

00:18:37.210 --> 00:18:42.800
weight is so great that they can dictate the
terms of economic relations or political relations

00:18:42.800 --> 00:18:49.820
with their neighbors is, I think, really out
of date. They sort of -- that had a plausibility

00:18:49.820 --> 00:18:53.650
20 years ago that it doesn't have anymore.

00:18:53.650 --> 00:18:58.921
GUFF: Before we go to member questions, I
want to hit on two of your other -- two of

00:18:58.921 --> 00:19:07.790
the other S's, Snowden, Syria, Sochi. So,
Syria. Will we see real progress on the Syria

00:19:07.790 --> 00:19:16.190
conflict? What, John, has Russia's role been?
And what do we think the outcome is going

00:19:16.190 --> 00:19:17.190
to be?

00:19:17.190 --> 00:19:23.020
BEYRLE: Well, I won't hazard a guess on the
outcome. I think that Russia's position on

00:19:23.020 --> 00:19:29.380
this has been governed largely by what Steve
talked about earlier, the sense that the United

00:19:29.380 --> 00:19:34.680
States really doesn't have the right to throw
its weight around in the world. And much of

00:19:34.680 --> 00:19:42.060
what motivated both President Medvedev and,
I think, especially President Putin on this

00:19:42.060 --> 00:19:48.740
was a very clear sense that Russia was going
to put a marker down that intervention, especially

00:19:48.740 --> 00:19:54.650
armed intervention, to change a government
was something that Russia, having acquiesced

00:19:54.650 --> 00:19:58.790
to that in the Libya case, would never again
acquiesce to.

00:19:58.790 --> 00:20:04.800
I think if you look at what Russia has done,
everything is very explainable, very understandable

00:20:04.800 --> 00:20:12.240
from that point of view. We've obviously found
a modus vivendi or modus labore (ph) now.

00:20:12.240 --> 00:20:16.540
I'm delighted to see that the two sides are
at least in the same room. I'm not sure if

00:20:16.540 --> 00:20:20.130
they're talking to each other or not, but
the fact that they're in the same room and

00:20:20.130 --> 00:20:25.520
the fact that the U.S. and Russia can take
credit for working together on an issue that,

00:20:25.520 --> 00:20:29.890
for the three years -- two years, at least,
that I was ambassador was the single most

00:20:29.890 --> 00:20:33.910
divisive issue in U.S.-Russia relations is
quite a step forward.

00:20:33.910 --> 00:20:42.920
GUFF: Angela, do you think that Putin and
Russia essentially bailed out the Obama administration

00:20:42.920 --> 00:20:44.010
on the Syria issue?

00:20:44.010 --> 00:20:45.010
STENT: I'll...

00:20:45.010 --> 00:20:47.610
GUFF: And is Russia at all vindicated on this
Syria issue?

00:20:47.610 --> 00:20:51.990
STENT: Well, I'll be a little bit more diplomatic,
and I will say that the U.S. government -- the

00:20:51.990 --> 00:20:58.630
White House hesitated and Russia stepped in.
And basically, I mean, I think Putin is a

00:20:58.630 --> 00:21:03.530
very smart politician. And I think he was
able to capitalize on the dithering in the

00:21:03.530 --> 00:21:08.560
West -- and I put the West together in general
-- about, do you use force, do you not use

00:21:08.560 --> 00:21:09.560
force?

00:21:09.560 --> 00:21:14.970
And I think to some extent, the Russian position
from the beginning was that Assad would prevail.

00:21:14.970 --> 00:21:19.570
They've done something to help him prevail.
And I think at the moment, it looks as if

00:21:19.570 --> 00:21:23.900
he may, at least for some time. And I think
the other part where they may be vindicated

00:21:23.900 --> 00:21:28.670
was also warning that a lot of the Syrian
opposition was controlled by extremist, radical,

00:21:28.670 --> 00:21:34.360
Al Qaida-linked forces, and we now find out
-- now find out that, in fact, that's largely

00:21:34.360 --> 00:21:35.360
true.

00:21:35.360 --> 00:21:40.341
So I think they're -- and they want strong
secular governments in this part of the world

00:21:40.341 --> 00:21:44.770
for their own reasons. And I think in -- in
a way, we're not that far apart on that, but

00:21:44.770 --> 00:21:48.280
obviously we've differed on how you get there.

00:21:48.280 --> 00:21:58.970
GUFF: Steve, on Iran, probably the largest
issue on the foreign policy level -- is this

00:21:58.970 --> 00:22:04.230
going to also overshadow any progress on arms
control? Has arms control now taken a back

00:22:04.230 --> 00:22:10.950
seat to Iran and denuclearizing -- potentially
denuclearizing Iran?

00:22:10.950 --> 00:22:16.020
SESTANOVICH: I think arms control could take
a back seat to just about any issue you could

00:22:16.020 --> 00:22:22.010
come up with in Russian-American relations,
because the Russians aren't very interested.

00:22:22.010 --> 00:22:33.070
This administration hoped that some kind of
arms control deal, a new START II, could be

00:22:33.070 --> 00:22:39.280
an instrument for reviving the reset. And
the Russians just made it clear they aren't

00:22:39.280 --> 00:22:46.790
particularly interested and have rebuffed
efforts to try to, you know, come up with

00:22:46.790 --> 00:22:54.080
that idea, rebuff suggestions by the Americans
that they take different pieces of it, try

00:22:54.080 --> 00:22:56.160
to make it work.

00:22:56.160 --> 00:23:04.850
Iran is a big issue for the Russians for a
lot of different reasons. They have pretty

00:23:04.850 --> 00:23:12.640
good relations with Iran. But they also have
the extra interest that we've been talking

00:23:12.640 --> 00:23:19.480
about off and on here, which is that a solution
is a way of preventing the use of American

00:23:19.480 --> 00:23:30.750
force, because American force is the big no-no
of relations between Moscow and Washington

00:23:30.750 --> 00:23:38.230
over all the postwar decades. And it is -- both
the postwar decades.

00:23:38.230 --> 00:23:45.040
It's what the Russians most want to avoid,
because it indicates their inability to prevent

00:23:45.040 --> 00:23:50.030
outcomes that they -- that they don't approve
of. It shows the meaninglessness of a U.N.

00:23:50.030 --> 00:23:56.760
veto and so forth. So there's a -- there's
a real interest on the part of the Russians

00:23:56.760 --> 00:24:02.800
to actually make this work, and I think it's
-- I mean, most American officials believe

00:24:02.800 --> 00:24:05.430
that the Russians have played a pretty constructive
role.

00:24:05.430 --> 00:24:09.060
BEYRLE: But it really has changed. When I
joined the foreign service in '83, went to

00:24:09.060 --> 00:24:14.600
Moscow on my first tour, arms control was
the center of gravity in the U.S.-Soviet relationship,

00:24:14.600 --> 00:24:18.770
no question about it. It's what everyone wanted
to do, because it's what mattered, and not

00:24:18.770 --> 00:24:23.540
for nothing, and it still matters, because
we're both armed to the teeth, 95 percent

00:24:23.540 --> 00:24:26.420
of the world's nuclear weapons, as we know.

00:24:26.420 --> 00:24:32.990
The problem is that as the arms control equation
has kind of receded, as the center of gravity,

00:24:32.990 --> 00:24:39.870
nothing's moved into its place. I think eventually
economics, trade and investment and economic

00:24:39.870 --> 00:24:44.390
partnerships will, but it's not there yet.
The advice that I give to the young foreign

00:24:44.390 --> 00:24:49.570
service officers I meet now is, don't study
arms control. Don't make that your specialty.

00:24:49.570 --> 00:24:55.230
Make your specialty the economics of energy,
and you'll have full employment in the 21st

00:24:55.230 --> 00:24:56.310
century.

00:24:56.310 --> 00:25:02.420
GUFF: One more question before we go to members.
Russian domestic politics, the demonstrations

00:25:02.420 --> 00:25:08.150
of a year, 18 months ago are now kind of a
distant memory, things much calmer in Moscow

00:25:08.150 --> 00:25:14.700
and the other cities. What does that say about
the relationship between government and the

00:25:14.700 --> 00:25:20.870
people right now in Russia? What does it say
about looking forward to the end of Putin's

00:25:20.870 --> 00:25:24.100
first term? His second first term?

00:25:24.100 --> 00:25:25.100
(LAUGHTER)

00:25:25.100 --> 00:25:28.500
STENT: His second -- well, I would say -- I
mean, I'll start off, maybe. I think Putin

00:25:28.500 --> 00:25:33.870
has quite successfully dealt with the kind
of liberal pro-Western left. I mean, in some

00:25:33.870 --> 00:25:39.530
ways, he's made enough concessions to them,
he's in a dialogue with them, and you don't

00:25:39.530 --> 00:25:44.400
see that many people on the streets anymore.
That part of the opposition is quieter. It's

00:25:44.400 --> 00:25:46.370
always divided, too.

00:25:46.370 --> 00:25:51.930
What I think he hasn't dealt well with in
the longer run is the nationalist opposition

00:25:51.930 --> 00:25:56.370
to him, or the people who don't like the people
from the Caucasus who work in, you know, the

00:25:56.370 --> 00:26:01.720
big cities and who are much more hard-line
and extreme. And that, I think, may come back

00:26:01.720 --> 00:26:06.490
to haunt him, as we see with all of the things
that are happening in the North Caucasus.

00:26:06.490 --> 00:26:12.090
But his popularity rates are up. The question
is, I -- you know, what'll make it much more

00:26:12.090 --> 00:26:16.610
difficult for him will be what happens in
the Russian economy, what happens to oil prices?

00:26:16.610 --> 00:26:21.460
If growth rates are at 1.4 percent for the
next few years, as they're predicted to be,

00:26:21.460 --> 00:26:23.120
then it makes it more difficult.

00:26:23.120 --> 00:26:24.260
GUFF: Steve?

00:26:24.260 --> 00:26:29.100
SESTANOVICH: There's an interesting -- interesting
poll today or the past couple of days showing

00:26:29.100 --> 00:26:35.740
that 68 percent of Russians would vote for
Putin today, but only 22 percent said they

00:26:35.740 --> 00:26:45.870
want him to run again in 2018, and 47 percent
said they would prefer somebody else. So this

00:26:45.870 --> 00:26:51.640
is a very common view, which actually you
probably could have gotten similar poll results

00:26:51.640 --> 00:26:59.750
in 2012. A lot of people in the Russian elite,
people who'd benefited from Putinism, felt

00:26:59.750 --> 00:27:05.230
this is a guy whose moment has passed and
for Russia to become a normal country, we

00:27:05.230 --> 00:27:11.610
need to be able to draw on new leaders, we
need a system in which we actually have replacement

00:27:11.610 --> 00:27:16.240
of leaders, where democratic processes become
a bit more meaningful.

00:27:16.240 --> 00:27:22.660
What does it mean that all of this -- all
the protest disappeared, that -- you know,

00:27:22.660 --> 00:27:30.150
you have had (inaudible) by the opposition
to local elections, you know? There are elections

00:27:30.150 --> 00:27:37.960
for the Moscow City Council this year, and
if you're a serious activist in (inaudible)

00:27:37.960 --> 00:27:42.170
that's what you're focusing on, gubernatorial
elections, as well.

00:27:42.170 --> 00:27:48.860
But it's amazing to me how much -- we think
we do our elections early -- how many Russians

00:27:48.860 --> 00:27:54.880
are already talking about 2018 and how much
their sense is, if only we could find some

00:27:54.880 --> 00:27:57.930
way to edge this guy out.

00:27:57.930 --> 00:28:07.480
GUFF: Good. We have 30 minutes of time for
member questions, so let me open up the floor

00:28:07.480 --> 00:28:13.750
to questions. I remind you, your questions
and the entire meeting is on-the-record. Please

00:28:13.750 --> 00:28:19.620
stand, state your name and affiliation, and
ask your question.

00:28:19.620 --> 00:28:20.930
Sir?

00:28:20.930 --> 00:28:31.570
QUESTION: Hi, I'm Leonid Kishkovsky of the
Orthodox Church in America and the World Conference

00:28:31.570 --> 00:28:39.070
of Religions for Peace. And I would like to
thank the panelists for extremely lucid and

00:28:39.070 --> 00:28:46.900
helpful commentary. Although Ukraine was mentioned,
it still is the elephant in the room, certainly

00:28:46.900 --> 00:28:55.010
as we meet today and this week. The position
of the foreign ministry and the government

00:28:55.010 --> 00:29:02.350
of Russia has been that they scrupulously
observe neutrality and do not interfere in

00:29:02.350 --> 00:29:11.800
internal affairs of Ukraine and that the West
has been interfering grossly and inappropriately.

00:29:11.800 --> 00:29:19.620
Now, the latter point may be true, but it's
been ineffective, whereas the more quiet interference

00:29:19.620 --> 00:29:26.770
of the Russian Federation seems to have been
effective, at least at the governmental level.

00:29:26.770 --> 00:29:37.880
I call friends in Ukraine a lot these days,
and they are convinced that the greatest influence

00:29:37.880 --> 00:29:42.860
on decisions by the president of Ukraine comes
from the Kremlin and Moscow.

00:29:42.860 --> 00:29:43.860
GUFF: Question is?

00:29:43.860 --> 00:29:52.610
QUESTION: And the question is, what are you
thinking -- the panelists -- about the Ukraine-Russia

00:29:52.610 --> 00:30:01.280
relationship, which I think has a lot of potential
impact on what happens in Europe and also

00:30:01.280 --> 00:30:05.980
what happens between the U.S. and Russia?

00:30:05.980 --> 00:30:11.320
BEYRLE: I think the overall problem, Father
Kishkovsky, is that there are still too many

00:30:11.320 --> 00:30:17.530
people in power in Russia who measure Russia's
power and influence the old-fashioned way,

00:30:17.530 --> 00:30:23.220
by how weak the neighbors are. That's not
a 21st century view. That's not a view that

00:30:23.220 --> 00:30:29.230
says if Ukraine or the Baltics or Central
Asia is a robust trading partner, that's good

00:30:29.230 --> 00:30:36.410
for Russia. But unfortunately, it's, for understandable
reasons, the way a lot of people still measure

00:30:36.410 --> 00:30:38.060
Russia's strength.

00:30:38.060 --> 00:30:42.680
I think the other problem with regard specifically
to Ukraine is there are still, unfortunately,

00:30:42.680 --> 00:30:48.990
too many people who don't see Ukraine as a
separate sovereign entity, whose concept of

00:30:48.990 --> 00:30:54.470
Russia, if you want to call them imperial
nostalgics, is inconceivable without Ukraine

00:30:54.470 --> 00:31:02.300
as a part of that. Until that knot is cut,
we're, I'm afraid, going to -- probably in

00:31:02.300 --> 00:31:09.120
for more disagreements and more tension, rather
than less.

00:31:09.120 --> 00:31:11.270
GUFF: Esther?

00:31:11.270 --> 00:31:18.960
QUESTION: Hi, Esther Dyson. I'd just love
to hear your comments on what Navalny published

00:31:18.960 --> 00:31:25.280
this morning. Is that going to change those
poll results that Steve mentioned? What else

00:31:25.280 --> 00:31:30.450
-- it's not that anybody's really surprised,
but seeing it all out in public I think has

00:31:30.450 --> 00:31:31.710
got to have some impact.

00:31:31.710 --> 00:31:34.650
GUFF: Can you just -- Esther, can you just
describe it for people who haven't seen it?

00:31:34.650 --> 00:31:35.650
STENT: Is this about the Sochi Olympics?

00:31:35.650 --> 00:31:43.620
QUESTION: Yeah, it's -- so Navalny, the opposition
leader who was in jail, let out of jail, whatever...

00:31:43.620 --> 00:31:45.000
SESTANOVICH: Ran for Moscow mayor.

00:31:45.000 --> 00:31:49.620
QUESTION: ... ran for Moscow mayor, much to
-- was allowed to run for Moscow mayor. That

00:31:49.620 --> 00:31:59.000
was the surprising part. On his website today,
published in amazing detail records, documents,

00:31:59.000 --> 00:32:07.690
data, about all the corruption and construction
and criminal enterprises and lack of completion

00:32:07.690 --> 00:32:10.350
of all the facilities in Sochi.

00:32:10.350 --> 00:32:15.980
STENT: I think -- I mean, I'll just start
off, maybe. I think a lot of Russians understand

00:32:15.980 --> 00:32:20.190
that there's been a lot of corruption and
they don't believe what the government says,

00:32:20.190 --> 00:32:24.721
anyway. And so -- and the people -- you know,
he's preaching to the choir. The people who

00:32:24.721 --> 00:32:28.500
support him are going to be outraged by this,
but they've been outraged, anyway.

00:32:28.500 --> 00:32:32.810
And I don't think it's going to make that
much difference. I mean, the figure -- Medvedev

00:32:32.810 --> 00:32:37.570
used the figure $51 billion. Putin apparently
used the figure of $7 billion at some point.

00:32:37.570 --> 00:32:42.090
Now Navalny says it's, what, $70 billion?
I don't think it'll make that much difference,

00:32:42.090 --> 00:32:46.620
because people -- you know, still, most Russians
would like those games to succeed, because

00:32:46.620 --> 00:32:48.650
it's going to be a very important moment.

00:32:48.650 --> 00:32:53.110
SESTANOVICH: I'd just add one thing. Corruption
is a kind of wild card in Russian politics.

00:32:53.110 --> 00:33:02.790
There is -- you can mobilize people with a
certain amount of outrage about this. "They're

00:33:02.790 --> 00:33:13.520
stealing from us." And that -- that has energized
people in the past. The poll results all show

00:33:13.520 --> 00:33:19.310
that. And Navalny is an interesting character,
because he's been able to do this kind of

00:33:19.310 --> 00:33:25.830
in the open. You know, he asks people to send
him information.

00:33:25.830 --> 00:33:29.691
They don't do anything -- they don't hire
private investigators. You know, they get

00:33:29.691 --> 00:33:36.470
most -- I mean, the Navalny model for exposing
corruption is just -- is...

00:33:36.470 --> 00:33:37.470
GUFF: Crowd-sourcing.

00:33:37.470 --> 00:33:43.320
SESTANOVICH: ... is to invite it. It's -- you
know, right, WikiLeaks. Good analogy.

00:33:43.320 --> 00:33:44.990
GUFF: Wikipedia.

00:33:44.990 --> 00:33:51.830
SESTANOVICH: And he has made himself a very
formidable character in Russian politics on

00:33:51.830 --> 00:33:57.950
this basis. They have sought to give him a
little running room when it has been convenient,

00:33:57.950 --> 00:34:03.610
but you should not think that he's going to
be in the clear completely.

00:34:03.610 --> 00:34:10.520
A little story about Navalny. He's registered
– has been elected as the leader of the

00:34:10.520 --> 00:34:15.740
People's Alliance Party. The Kremlin quickly,
after he was elected leader of the People's

00:34:15.740 --> 00:34:20.030
Alliance Party, registered another People's
Alliance Party and now won't register his,

00:34:20.030 --> 00:34:22.249
because it's got the same name as somebody
else.

00:34:22.249 --> 00:34:28.679
So the -- you know, they are -- they're working
on him. They're closing off these avenues.

00:34:28.679 --> 00:34:34.990
So I wouldn't -- I just wouldn't rule out
the idea that you can mobilize people in an

00:34:34.990 --> 00:34:36.190
angry way against...

00:34:36.190 --> 00:34:37.190
(CROSSTALK)

00:34:37.190 --> 00:34:40.169
GUFF: You think we're beyond it. OK.

00:34:40.169 --> 00:34:41.210
Please?

00:34:41.210 --> 00:34:48.220
QUESTION: Marta Varela, formerly of Hunter
College. A follow-up on Sochi. The host country

00:34:48.220 --> 00:34:54.060
has, in many years, that they've acted as
the sponsor of the Olympics gotten a kind

00:34:54.060 --> 00:34:59.019
of bounce from that. Now, you know, as the
former speaker said, there is a great deal

00:34:59.019 --> 00:35:03.580
of knowledge that the Olympics have involved
a great deal of corruption. Will Putin get

00:35:03.580 --> 00:35:09.220
some kind of Sochi bounce from this? And if
so, how long will it last? And is it going

00:35:09.220 --> 00:35:15.240
to be something effective in terms of his
doing exactly what you spoke of, Steve, exerting

00:35:15.240 --> 00:35:19.109
the will in a particularly Russian form on
events?

00:35:19.109 --> 00:35:23.220
QUESTION: OK, Sochi bounce. I think everyone
needs to comment on that one.

00:35:23.220 --> 00:35:28.749
STENT: I mean, I'll start off. So the -- I
mean, the reason Putin obviously wanted these

00:35:28.749 --> 00:35:34.700
Olympics and he got them, in 2007 and, really,
when Russia was doing fantastically well economically

00:35:34.700 --> 00:35:38.369
-- I mean, it was before the crash -- was
to show that not only Russia is back, but

00:35:38.369 --> 00:35:42.600
it can build a world-class tourist resort,
it's going to excel in the games, the athletes

00:35:42.600 --> 00:35:44.430
are going to do well.

00:35:44.430 --> 00:35:49.369
And some of that has already become tarnished
before it happened. One of it -- one thing

00:35:49.369 --> 00:35:54.640
has to do with the corruption and the realization
and the questioning about whether these facilities

00:35:54.640 --> 00:35:59.150
are going to be completed and in what shape
they're going to be. Then there's, of course,

00:35:59.150 --> 00:36:03.049
the question of the sub-tropical climate,
so one hopes they have enough snow, but maybe

00:36:03.049 --> 00:36:04.049
they do.

00:36:04.049 --> 00:36:08.259
And then you have to ask yourself why they
passed this legislation before the Sochi Olympics,

00:36:08.259 --> 00:36:14.549
you know, the so-called homosexual propaganda
legislation, that's obviously caused an uproar.

00:36:14.549 --> 00:36:18.359
It's alienated people. It's added an entire
dimension to this which they really didn't

00:36:18.359 --> 00:36:20.249
need.

00:36:20.249 --> 00:36:24.290
And then he's put himself forward, as we've
already heard from Steve now, that Russia

00:36:24.290 --> 00:36:28.700
is the leader, if you like, of the new conservative
international, that it represents traditional

00:36:28.700 --> 00:36:34.270
Christian values, which the West doesn't.
I'm not sure when the Sochi games are over

00:36:34.270 --> 00:36:38.252
whether -- I mean, what kind of a bounce it
will give him. He would also like Sochi now

00:36:38.252 --> 00:36:43.970
to be a sort of world-class tourist resort
that people will go to in the future, remains

00:36:43.970 --> 00:36:44.970
to be seen.

00:36:44.970 --> 00:36:48.581
I mean, even if there are no terrorist incidents,
which hopefully there won't be, and there

00:36:48.581 --> 00:36:53.190
are no other, you know, incidents that have
to do with protests against Russia's laws,

00:36:53.190 --> 00:36:57.019
I'm not sure that it will give him that bounce,
because of all the other things that have

00:36:57.019 --> 00:36:58.019
preceded it.

00:36:58.019 --> 00:37:03.499
BEYRLE: I think that even though Russians
do list corruption as their number-one concern

00:37:03.499 --> 00:37:08.470
about the country when asked by pollsters,
it's the kind of corruption that makes them

00:37:08.470 --> 00:37:12.390
have to pay $25 to get the sheets changed
at the hospital where their grandfather's

00:37:12.390 --> 00:37:19.390
staying. Corruption on the scale of $70 billion
that Navalny maybe rightly points out I don't

00:37:19.390 --> 00:37:24.140
think is going to really redound to Putin's
discredit.

00:37:24.140 --> 00:37:30.430
More discrediting, I think, would be an Olympics
which is judged by the outside world as a

00:37:30.430 --> 00:37:36.900
failure or is lampooned as, "Nice try, but
you just really didn't manage to do anything."

00:37:36.900 --> 00:37:42.250
Putin's trump argument will be, as Angela
said, this wasn't $70 billion spent to stage

00:37:42.250 --> 00:37:49.849
an Olympics. It was $70 billion invested in
the infrastructure of our main southern resort,

00:37:49.849 --> 00:37:53.910
and that's as good for the Russian people,
who want to take a vacation there, as for

00:37:53.910 --> 00:37:56.099
the Bulgarians or the Germans.

00:37:56.099 --> 00:37:57.569
QUESTION: Sochi bounce?

00:37:57.569 --> 00:38:02.710
SESTANOVICH: You know, pocketbook issues are
going to be more important than the Sochi

00:38:02.710 --> 00:38:08.780
bounce. You know, we remember the '69 Mets
here, but I don't think that's what's going

00:38:08.780 --> 00:38:09.780
to happen.

00:38:09.780 --> 00:38:20.740
You know, you've got an economic slowdown
in Russia that is pretty disturbing to a lot

00:38:20.740 --> 00:38:28.059
of people in the policy elite, trying to figure
out what to do about it. I think it's -- it's

00:38:28.059 --> 00:38:34.190
the first time in a long time you had Russian
growth slower than America two years in a

00:38:34.190 --> 00:38:39.279
row. For -- you know, Russia grew twice as
fast as in the United States in the past decade.

00:38:39.279 --> 00:38:42.190
It's now growing more slowly.

00:38:42.190 --> 00:38:53.490
That is a threat to Putinism in a way that
-- probably greater than, you know, the exposure

00:38:53.490 --> 00:38:58.859
of corruption. That's what he's got to deal
with the day after Sochi. That's stuff -- he's

00:38:58.859 --> 00:39:01.520
got a full plate of problems to deal with.

00:39:01.520 --> 00:39:07.589
GUFF: I'm very surprised, actually, at all
the responses. The American public's view

00:39:07.589 --> 00:39:13.050
of Russia is -- seems to be still locked in
the -- in the Cold War...

00:39:13.050 --> 00:39:15.359
SESTANOVICH: You're calling us the American
public...

00:39:15.359 --> 00:39:16.359
(CROSSTALK)

00:39:16.359 --> 00:39:17.359
(LAUGHTER)

00:39:17.359 --> 00:39:18.359
BEYRLE: Or are you contrasting?

00:39:18.359 --> 00:39:23.549
GUFF: But no one feels that when the lights
go on, and NBC starts to show the games and

00:39:23.549 --> 00:39:29.970
patriotism starts and medals are given out,
that this view of Russia will change and become

00:39:29.970 --> 00:39:30.970
a little more modernized?

00:39:30.970 --> 00:39:31.970
BEYRLE: In this country?

00:39:31.970 --> 00:39:32.970
SESTANOVICH: Oh, you mean the outside view...

00:39:32.970 --> 00:39:33.970
QUESTION: In this country. Yeah, in America.

00:39:33.970 --> 00:39:34.970
STENT: In this country.

00:39:34.970 --> 00:39:39.940
SESTANOVICH: Oh. That kind of bounce, I can't
predict. Inside Russia, you know, we should

00:39:39.940 --> 00:39:47.349
remember that morning in America in 1984 was
connected to the '84 Olympics in Los Angeles.

00:39:47.349 --> 00:39:53.349
There was a kind of national euphoria over
that. You can't rule that sort of thing out.

00:39:53.349 --> 00:39:57.690
BEYRLE: Which the Soviets boycotted, if I
recall.

00:39:57.690 --> 00:40:07.410
GUFF: OK, next question, please, with the
-- your glasses, yes, and the necklace.

00:40:07.410 --> 00:40:11.769
QUESTION: Hi, my name's (OFF-MIKE) I'm a former
Sovietologist (OFF-MIKE)

00:40:11.769 --> 00:40:12.769
(LAUGHTER)

00:40:12.769 --> 00:40:13.769
BEYRLE: We're all -- we're all unemployed
ex-Sovietologists.

00:40:13.769 --> 00:40:14.769
(CROSSTALK)

00:40:14.769 --> 00:40:19.269
QUESTION: (OFF-MIKE) nice to see some employed
ones. My question is about civil society and

00:40:19.269 --> 00:40:24.170
gays, really. And so Khodorkovsky let out,
Pussy Riot let out. Like, this week it's OK

00:40:24.170 --> 00:40:29.880
to be gay in Russia. What happens post-Sochi?
Will there be any positive -- what's your

00:40:29.880 --> 00:40:34.890
prediction for post-Sochi, both on gay rights
and also on civil society, freedom of speech,

00:40:34.890 --> 00:40:36.519
those types of issues?

00:40:36.519 --> 00:40:41.420
STENT: Maybe -- can I start off with that?
I -- I think that -- I mean, this legislation

00:40:41.420 --> 00:40:46.980
that was passed, it has the backing -- the
strong backing of the Russian Orthodox Church.

00:40:46.980 --> 00:40:52.900
It has -- it's also trying to appeal to the
Muslim population of Russia. I've heard Putin

00:40:52.900 --> 00:40:57.960
say, you know, these are traditional Christian
values, but they're also traditional Muslim

00:40:57.960 --> 00:41:03.000
values. Putin the other day gave an interview
on one of our talk shows saying 70 countries

00:41:03.000 --> 00:41:07.859
in the world have similar legislation, and
7 of them behead people for this, by the way.

00:41:07.859 --> 00:41:14.930
So I think this is part of a longer-term -- this
is how this current Kremlin has staked out

00:41:14.930 --> 00:41:19.930
its appeal both domestically and abroad, that
this isn't part of Russian culture and you

00:41:19.930 --> 00:41:25.019
shouldn't force it on us. And I also think
the release of Khodorkovsky and the Pussy

00:41:25.019 --> 00:41:29.880
Riot people, I think that was largely done
to sort of preempt criticism at the Sochi

00:41:29.880 --> 00:41:36.400
Olympics. He let them out. He let the Greenpeace
people out. It doesn't really auger any liberalization.

00:41:36.400 --> 00:41:38.220
I would be very surprised if that was so.

00:41:38.220 --> 00:41:44.099
SESTANOVICH: Just a word, though, about civil
society in general, because I would say that

00:41:44.099 --> 00:41:55.559
we tend to exaggerate Putin's success in repressing
civil society groups that are involved in

00:41:55.559 --> 00:42:02.069
all kinds of activism, environmental activism,
you name the issue. And those people have

00:42:02.069 --> 00:42:13.339
a tougher time getting funds from abroad,
but they are still able to do it, and they've

00:42:13.339 --> 00:42:21.289
got a lot of mission and a lot of enthusiasm
and a lot of ability to operate, really, across

00:42:21.289 --> 00:42:26.140
Russia in the big cities and smaller ones.

00:42:26.140 --> 00:42:36.650
I think there's a -- what I hear from Russians
is, don't count us out. We are much more resilient

00:42:36.650 --> 00:42:44.079
than Putin thinks and able to organize and
take action in a way that he's going to be

00:42:44.079 --> 00:42:50.539
surprised by. So I -- there's a kind of confidence
and ebullience there that I -- I think we

00:42:50.539 --> 00:42:52.150
should not lose sight of.

00:42:52.150 --> 00:42:55.059
GUFF: Uh-huh, question in the very back?

00:42:55.059 --> 00:43:03.019
QUESTION: Hi, my name is Contessa Bourbon
from the New York Times. I'd like to ask,

00:43:03.019 --> 00:43:12.180
how optimistic are you that a new reset for
U.S. and Russian relationship will take place

00:43:12.180 --> 00:43:18.520
in the aftermath of Sochi Olympics, considering
that they are cooperating in security matters

00:43:18.520 --> 00:43:20.869
to prevent terrorist attacks?

00:43:20.869 --> 00:43:28.880
BEYRLE: Well, I'll try it. You said, how confident
am I that a new reset will take place in the

00:43:28.880 --> 00:43:29.880
U.S.-Russia...

00:43:29.880 --> 00:43:31.369
GUFF: A fifth -- a fifth reset.

00:43:31.369 --> 00:43:36.491
BEYRLE: A fifth reset? Very confident. Because
the history of U.S.-Russia relations is a

00:43:36.491 --> 00:43:43.540
never-ending boom-and-bust cycle, series of
boom-and-bust cycles. I just saw this week

00:43:43.540 --> 00:43:50.109
that the International Space Station has been
nominated for the Nobel Peace Prize next year.

00:43:50.109 --> 00:43:56.030
This is a joint Russian-American project which
will lead to, if it actually happens, if it's

00:43:56.030 --> 00:44:01.539
granted the Nobel Peace Prize, a tremendous
outpouring of good feeling on both sides.

00:44:01.539 --> 00:44:06.280
The Americans and Russians are working together
more in Syria than we were a year ago, as

00:44:06.280 --> 00:44:12.380
I pointed out earlier. And I think the -- the
figures on trade and investment, especially

00:44:12.380 --> 00:44:17.980
the American investment in Russia, the success
of American companies in Russia, also augers

00:44:17.980 --> 00:44:24.470
well for a bit of an uptick from what I think
is probably a low point that we hit maybe

00:44:24.470 --> 00:44:27.119
a year, 14 months ago in the relationship.

00:44:27.119 --> 00:44:28.329
But then it'll go down again.

00:44:28.329 --> 00:44:29.329
(LAUGHTER)

00:44:29.329 --> 00:44:34.670
STENT: So the real answer is, it would be
much better if there were no more resets.

00:44:34.670 --> 00:44:39.170
I mean, we've had this rollercoaster relationship
with Russia. It's much better to stand back

00:44:39.170 --> 00:44:43.999
and say, we work on some areas very well together.
There are other areas where we don't work

00:44:43.999 --> 00:44:48.769
that well together. And as long as the -- we
have the current sort of system that exists

00:44:48.769 --> 00:44:53.960
in Russia and the attitudes towards each other,
we shouldn't keep trying to find some golden

00:44:53.960 --> 00:44:59.019
key to a much better relationship, because
that then leads to disappointment. The expectations

00:44:59.019 --> 00:45:02.069
are wrong. The relationship has to be much
more realistic.

00:45:02.069 --> 00:45:06.019
GUFF: Yes, with the -- holding the paper?

00:45:06.019 --> 00:45:12.650
QUESTION: Thank you. Laurie Garrett from the
Council. I think until the Boston Marathon,

00:45:12.650 --> 00:45:18.230
the average American could not have told you
what part of the planet Dagestan was on, and

00:45:18.230 --> 00:45:22.420
probably most Americans could not on a map
tell you where Chechnya is, and certainly

00:45:22.420 --> 00:45:28.520
not Abkhazia or any of the heavily divisive
areas.

00:45:28.520 --> 00:45:33.460
So they're hearing on the news about black
widows, and they hear the U.S. Olympics Committee

00:45:33.460 --> 00:45:40.089
says don't wear anything that looks American
if you're in Sochi, and that even the athletes,

00:45:40.089 --> 00:45:46.950
when they're not in the village or in competition,
are not to wear anything that looks American,

00:45:46.950 --> 00:45:55.119
explain to us, why is this incredibly powerful,
almost police state, however you want to characterize

00:45:55.119 --> 00:46:02.630
Putin's government, unable to control and
stifle these separatist movements? And how

00:46:02.630 --> 00:46:09.579
much are Americans outside of the Boston Marathon
likely to be targeted by them?

00:46:09.579 --> 00:46:16.049
SESTANOVICH: I was a little surprised by the
suggestion that Americans were targets. And

00:46:16.049 --> 00:46:23.890
it may be that that is the expression of some
kind of intelligence that somebody has picked

00:46:23.890 --> 00:46:32.989
up that some particular group or individual
has got, you know, an aim to target Americans.

00:46:32.989 --> 00:46:48.410
But I think the purposes of the -- you know,
the Caucasus Emirate groups, the sort of -- the

00:46:48.410 --> 00:46:53.019
terrorist groups that have been operating
in the Caucasus for some time is -- and outside,

00:46:53.019 --> 00:46:59.180
I mean, you know, bombing in other -- bombings
in other cities, they aren't making Americans

00:46:59.180 --> 00:47:07.529
their targets. And I would -- I'd be a little
surprised to discover that that's the nature

00:47:07.529 --> 00:47:12.660
of the operations that they're -- that they're
conducting.

00:47:12.660 --> 00:47:20.489
The -- you know, some people have asked whether
-- how damaging it will be for Putin to -- if

00:47:20.489 --> 00:47:28.619
there is some kind of terrorist attack. I
think the -- it will be embarrassing, and

00:47:28.619 --> 00:47:35.250
it will be a revelation of the limits of his
power. But his response to it will be what

00:47:35.250 --> 00:47:41.290
it has been in almost all other cases, and
that is to extend his power, to make the case

00:47:41.290 --> 00:47:47.729
that this sort of attack requires a concentration
of power in his hands, and a strengthening

00:47:47.729 --> 00:47:56.009
of the state, and an ability to act against
its enemies. So that -- and there's just no

00:47:56.009 --> 00:48:06.339
doubt about that. That will be -- that will
be the opportunity, as he sees it, if there's

00:48:06.339 --> 00:48:08.829
an attack.

00:48:08.829 --> 00:48:13.809
GUFF: Left side, please?

00:48:13.809 --> 00:48:19.499
QUESTION: Thanks. I'm Kimberly Marten from
Barnard College at Columbia University. I'm

00:48:19.499 --> 00:48:22.700
wondering if each of you could say a little
bit more about the Russian relationship with

00:48:22.700 --> 00:48:28.329
China. Steve talked about Chinese energy investments
or investments in general in Central Asia.

00:48:28.329 --> 00:48:32.849
It had looked a few years ago like there might
be Russia-Chinese competition in Central Asia,

00:48:32.849 --> 00:48:37.440
but it appears more recently as though Russia
is almost falling in line behind China.

00:48:37.440 --> 00:48:42.630
So what do we think the Russia-China relationship
will look like in, let's say, a decade?

00:48:42.630 --> 00:48:48.039
BEYRLE: Well, I think the -- from the Russian
point of view, the China relationship is arguably

00:48:48.039 --> 00:48:53.829
the most important one that they have to manage,
maybe in this century, certainly for the next

00:48:53.829 --> 00:48:59.809
10 to 20 years, and it stems from that imbalance,
demographic and economic imbalance that you

00:48:59.809 --> 00:49:06.789
see on the border between Siberian Russia
far east and the parts of China that are pushed

00:49:06.789 --> 00:49:13.670
up against, where you've got 220 million Chinese
and only about 8 million Russians. I mean,

00:49:13.670 --> 00:49:22.729
they're mirror images of each other. The Russian
land is bereft of people and full of resources,

00:49:22.729 --> 00:49:27.640
and the Chinese land just across the border
is exactly the opposite, no resources and

00:49:27.640 --> 00:49:29.849
nothing but people.

00:49:29.849 --> 00:49:34.270
Managing that is the biggest single challenge
that, when I meet with thoughtful Russians,

00:49:34.270 --> 00:49:39.270
they identify after they get done talking
about the E.U. and NATO enlargement. They

00:49:39.270 --> 00:49:46.480
start talking about that. And the idea that
somehow Russia and China are fated to be allies

00:49:46.480 --> 00:49:50.380
somehow against us or against the Europeans,
if you -- as you pointed out, if you look

00:49:50.380 --> 00:49:55.700
at the parts of the world that matter most
to both countries, they're rivals, China and

00:49:55.700 --> 00:50:03.010
Russia. Central Asia, if Russia is falling
in line behind China, that's going to rile

00:50:03.010 --> 00:50:08.299
a lot of people in Russia, because they still
see part of Central Asia as their backyard.

00:50:08.299 --> 00:50:13.720
Meanwhile, the Russians in places like Vietnam
are helping the Vietnamese develop their offshore

00:50:13.720 --> 00:50:20.170
energy resources in waters that the Chinese
say are theirs. So I think we all have a lot

00:50:20.170 --> 00:50:24.490
of work to do to make sure that the rise of
China's managed in a way that keeps China

00:50:24.490 --> 00:50:29.819
playing by international rules, but Russia
in particular has to manage that relationship

00:50:29.819 --> 00:50:33.380
carefully, and right now the only way it can
do it is politically.

00:50:33.380 --> 00:50:38.650
STENT: Yeah, I was in China a couple of years
ago talking to Chinese Russia experts about

00:50:38.650 --> 00:50:42.910
all of this. And the Chinese are very clear-eyed
about everything. They say, Russia supports

00:50:42.910 --> 00:50:49.160
us on all the important foreign policy issues
for us, Taiwan, Tibet, a multipolar world.

00:50:49.160 --> 00:50:50.190
We can work together.

00:50:50.190 --> 00:50:55.400
Then you start talking to them about the domestic
system in Russia, how Russia is governed or

00:50:55.400 --> 00:51:00.579
not governed, and they were pretty critical.
If -- I mean, the Chinese see this very much

00:51:00.579 --> 00:51:05.940
as -- you know, to quote a colleague of mine,
an access of convenience. It's very useful

00:51:05.940 --> 00:51:09.910
for them. It's instrumental for them. They
need the Russian energy. They work with Russia.

00:51:09.910 --> 00:51:14.380
Again, they support each other in the United
Nations Security Council, but there's no kind

00:51:14.380 --> 00:51:17.289
of romantic notion of a great strategic partnership.

00:51:17.289 --> 00:51:22.319
I think on the Russian side, it's very complicated,
because, on the one hand, they also see this

00:51:22.319 --> 00:51:26.729
as an important partnership with China, again,
against, you know, the Western -- if you read

00:51:26.729 --> 00:51:30.490
their foreign policy doctrines, you know,
the number-one enemy is still NATO and the

00:51:30.490 --> 00:51:35.630
United States. And you can't even talk about
China publicly in Russia in that vein.

00:51:35.630 --> 00:51:40.549
But on the other hand, as John said, I mean,
you go to the Russian far east, and it's quite

00:51:40.549 --> 00:51:45.009
clear what the dynamics are there. And in
the long run, the Russians have to be very

00:51:45.009 --> 00:51:48.490
concerned about this, and I don't think they
know how to manage that, either.

00:51:48.490 --> 00:51:53.900
SESTANOVICH: Just one word on this. For the
Russians that -- you know, China is a fabulous

00:51:53.900 --> 00:52:03.220
market and opportunity to develop a relationship
that pays off economically. The problem is

00:52:03.220 --> 00:52:09.229
letting themselves be in the position where
they don't have any other friends. And that's

00:52:09.229 --> 00:52:13.420
not a good relationship to have with the Chinese.

00:52:13.420 --> 00:52:21.500
And they are working a little bit at some
of the networking that they need to do in

00:52:21.500 --> 00:52:28.400
order to make sure that they are not all by
themselves against the Chinese. So you see

00:52:28.400 --> 00:52:34.299
some cultivation of the Japanese. They've
really worked at getting Prime Minister Abe

00:52:34.299 --> 00:52:44.740
to come to the games. There is an awareness
that being -- being all by yourself in the

00:52:44.740 --> 00:52:48.559
-- in a junior partner relationship with the
Chinese may not be so great.

00:52:48.559 --> 00:52:53.809
GUFF: Yes, please, front row?

00:52:53.809 --> 00:53:00.160
QUESTION: Bill Drozdiak, American Council
on Germany. Do you think there's a conscious

00:53:00.160 --> 00:53:06.690
and deliberate strategy on the part of Putin
to use the Snowden revelations to damage and

00:53:06.690 --> 00:53:13.059
drive a wedge between the United States and
the European allies? Or is it just opportunism

00:53:13.059 --> 00:53:14.819
that he's taking advantage of?

00:53:14.819 --> 00:53:22.059
And, secondly, just -- would it be in American
interests to export a lot of the shale oil

00:53:22.059 --> 00:53:27.200
gas we're producing in order to drive down
the price and put pressure on Putin?

00:53:27.200 --> 00:53:28.200
BEYRLE: Let me try...

00:53:28.200 --> 00:53:29.349
STENT: I'll take -- oh, sorry.

00:53:29.349 --> 00:53:30.349
BEYRLE: OK. You want Snowden?

00:53:30.349 --> 00:53:32.180
STENT: Can I start with Snowden?

00:53:32.180 --> 00:53:34.690
GUFF: Start with Snowden, and then, John,
please jump in.

00:53:34.690 --> 00:53:38.479
STENT: I mean, in -- in the old days of the
Cold War, right, we'd all talk about the Soviet

00:53:38.479 --> 00:53:42.580
Union is trying to drive wedges between, you
know, the United States and its European allies.

00:53:42.580 --> 00:53:47.589
I mean, I haven't -- I can't remember a time
when U.S.-German relations -- I mean, they

00:53:47.589 --> 00:53:52.160
haven't been this fraught for a very long
time, and this is all thanks to Edward Snowden.

00:53:52.160 --> 00:53:56.160
So if Putin had dreamed something up, I think
he couldn't have dreamed something better,

00:53:56.160 --> 00:54:00.411
in terms of everyone shocked, we know there's
a lot of hypocrisy in all of this, but at

00:54:00.411 --> 00:54:03.989
least outwardly everyone has to be shocked
that these things are happening.

00:54:03.989 --> 00:54:09.809
And, you know, one can debate how deliberate
this was, but it certainly worked out pretty

00:54:09.809 --> 00:54:14.799
well on that score, in terms of, you know,
the problems between the U.S., particularly,

00:54:14.799 --> 00:54:19.619
and Germany now, and this is not going away.
And you may have -- I'm sure you followed

00:54:19.619 --> 00:54:24.539
his latest interview yesterday on -- with
Hubert Seipel, I think, where, you know -- and

00:54:24.539 --> 00:54:28.289
he reveals more and more about all the terrible
things we're doing to Germany. So this -- you

00:54:28.289 --> 00:54:31.119
know, it's almost a fantasy, I would think,
for someone in the Kremlin.

00:54:31.119 --> 00:54:36.799
BEYRLE: I agree that this is mostly opportunism
on Putin's part, and I think it's mostly run

00:54:36.799 --> 00:54:42.749
its course. Snowden now is a wasting asset,
if he ever was an asset for the Russians.

00:54:42.749 --> 00:54:49.000
The benefit that Putin got from sheltering
Snowden and, in essence, sticking a finger

00:54:49.000 --> 00:54:56.469
in the American eye will pay negative dividends
in greater measure in the months to come,

00:54:56.469 --> 00:55:01.480
and that's why I don't think after the one-year
temporary asylum that Snowden was granted

00:55:01.480 --> 00:55:05.619
by Putin that this will be extended, and you
can even see this a bit in some of the things

00:55:05.619 --> 00:55:09.019
that both Putin and Snowden are beginning
to say.

00:55:09.019 --> 00:55:14.829
STENT: But not Pushkov, huh? He said he can
stay there as long as he likes.

00:55:14.829 --> 00:55:22.809
BEYRLE: Not running the country, last I checked.

00:55:22.809 --> 00:55:27.809
GUFF: Yes, please?

00:55:27.809 --> 00:55:39.019
QUESTION: Norma Globeman (ph), ex-UNDP. President
Obama has three years almost left to go, two

00:55:39.019 --> 00:55:48.859
years of which will be lame-duckism, but never
mind. What is your view of the state of the

00:55:48.859 --> 00:56:00.049
relationship with Putin now, the real state
of the relationship, and to what degree will

00:56:00.049 --> 00:56:08.170
it be a potentially useful or an impediment
-- how serious an impediment, if it is an

00:56:08.170 --> 00:56:10.349
impediment, over the next few years?

00:56:10.349 --> 00:56:16.710
GUFF: Well, this is probably good questions
to close on. We're approaching the witching

00:56:16.710 --> 00:56:22.890
hour of 7 o'clock, so let's -- why don't we
wrap up with some closing thoughts on that

00:56:22.890 --> 00:56:23.890
topic?

00:56:23.890 --> 00:56:29.810
SESTANOVICH: Bill Clinton, when he went to
Moscow in 2000 and Putin has just become president,

00:56:29.810 --> 00:56:36.829
said that his feeling about Putin was that
he was like somebody looking over the shoulder

00:56:36.829 --> 00:56:40.170
of the person he's talking to at a cocktail
party and looking for somebody more interesting

00:56:40.170 --> 00:56:49.450
who's coming in. And before too long, Putin
is going to be doing that to Barack Obama.

00:56:49.450 --> 00:56:54.779
Barack Obama doesn't seem to like it very
much. And I would say, if there's been a downgrading

00:56:54.779 --> 00:57:02.950
of the relationship on the U.S. side, probably
been as much the president's doing as anybody

00:57:02.950 --> 00:57:04.500
else.

00:57:04.500 --> 00:57:11.390
You're right that lame-duckism is a -- is
a problem for American presidents. It hasn't

00:57:11.390 --> 00:57:16.609
kept American presidents from reaching major
agreements with Russian presidents in their

00:57:16.609 --> 00:57:24.630
last year or two in office. But I'm not sure
that either side really has an agenda that

00:57:24.630 --> 00:57:31.270
will yield that kind of agreement. They're
not into reset-ism. This is more -- has become

00:57:31.270 --> 00:57:32.710
a more transactional relationship.

00:57:32.710 --> 00:57:36.690
STENT: I think, you know, in the beginning,
President Obama, this was a very high priority

00:57:36.690 --> 00:57:42.920
for him. We had the reset. Then, when Putin
and Medvedev switched jobs, it got much more

00:57:42.920 --> 00:57:47.019
difficult. I personally -- I agree with Steve
-- I don't think this a high priority for

00:57:47.019 --> 00:57:51.469
the White House. I don't think there's much
on the bilateral agenda for the next few years.

00:57:51.469 --> 00:57:54.990
The economic relationship we will try and
push, and we're trying to do that. They're

00:57:54.990 --> 00:57:59.400
not interested in any more arms control. So
I think you're going to see generally Russia

00:57:59.400 --> 00:58:04.789
taking a lower -- it already has priority,
except in those areas, international, multilateral

00:58:04.789 --> 00:58:08.619
ones, where we will work together. But bilaterally,
I think not much.

00:58:08.619 --> 00:58:15.969
BEYRLE: I think there's always a tendency
to overanalyze the body language between leaders.

00:58:15.969 --> 00:58:21.719
I mean, having sat in meetings with Bill Clinton
and Boris Yeltsin, I can tell you, the body

00:58:21.719 --> 00:58:24.329
language in that -- some of those meetings
wasn't very good, either.

00:58:24.329 --> 00:58:25.519
GUFF: But it sells books.

00:58:25.519 --> 00:58:30.339
BEYRLE: But it depends on which picture you
choose. In the end, both leaders are very

00:58:30.339 --> 00:58:34.380
pragmatic. Putin has showed that pragmatism
again. He showed it at the end of the Bush

00:58:34.380 --> 00:58:41.529
administration, the Bush 43 administration,
and I think, at the end of the day, this is

00:58:41.529 --> 00:58:45.660
an important relationship for both countries.
It has to be, simply because of the special

00:58:45.660 --> 00:58:47.920
place that we both occupy.

00:58:47.920 --> 00:58:52.839
And we really -- and I think both leaders
realize this -- don't have the luxury to downgrade

00:58:52.839 --> 00:58:58.329
the relationship to the degree that I think
the press sometimes accuses us of having done.

00:58:58.329 --> 00:59:04.130
GUFF: Well, a big thank you to our panelists
tonight. I want to make one reminder.

00:59:04.130 --> 00:59:05.559
(APPLAUSE)

00:59:05.559 --> 00:59:14.729
Yeah, one very important reminder. Angela
Stent's book is available for sale right outside

00:59:14.729 --> 00:59:21.329
our doors here and available on Amazon.com.
Steve Sestanovich's book comes out in preview

00:59:21.329 --> 00:59:26.390
on Amazon.com, called "Maximalist." Please
take a look at it. Thank you very much.

00:59:26.390 --> 00:59:27.390
BEYRLE: And I'm still writing.

00:59:27.390 --> 00:59:28.390
(LAUGHTER)

00:59:28.390 --> 00:59:28.391
(APPLAUSE)

